Draw a **green slime enemy**, a 6-frame **sprite sheet** of one squash-and-stretch
hop loop, using only the drawing tool.

The slime is a cute, soft, jelly-like green blob with two simple eyes — a common
little enemy that bounces along the ground. You are drawing a single bounce loop:
it squashes wide on the ground, stretches tall as it launches, rounds off at the
apex, stretches as it drops, and squashes on landing, then repeats. Draw it across
frames 0–5 on full transparency, with two readable eyes riding the body throughout.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. It
is the sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue — recorded to a per-frame action log — are the **authoritative output that
is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced any other way (writing a
PNG directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the
recorded operations count.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-circle --frame 0 --cx 16 --cy 20 --r 9 --color "#57c04a"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the slime up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png` matches
the brief: one cute green jelly blob with two eyes, drawn across the six phases of
a springy hop — squashed on the ground, stretched into a launch, rounded at the
apex, stretched on the drop, and squashed on landing — all on full transparency and
looping cleanly. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs
are your finished submission.
